@page "/markdown"
@inject ISednaUi Ui

<CatHead Title="Markdown" Tier="@CatHead.Tier3">
    Two independent pieces: <code>.markdown-body</code> styles rendered Markdown wherever it
    appears, and <code>.md-editor</code> is a toolbar + textarea + live preview driven by
    <code>sednaUi.md</code>, which also holds the renderer, <code>sednaUi.md.render()</code>.
</CatHead>

<CatGroup Title="Rendered Markdown">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MarkdownBody" Title="Rendered content" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            Put <code>.markdown-body</code> on the container holding rendered HTML. It normalises
            headings, lists, code, quotes and links onto the tokens, and strips the first and last
            margin so it sits flush in a card body.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MarkdownTable" Title="Tables and nested lists" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            A table in rendered Markdown gets <a href="/table"><code>.table</code></a>'s own borders and
            header treatment without the class, and nested lists indent one step per level. The HTML here
            comes from somewhere else, so a long <code>&lt;code&gt;</code> breaks inside a word and a
            wide table scrolls rather than widening the page.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MdSyntax" Title="Everything it reads" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live below</strong> &mdash; edit the left, read the right. <code>sednaUi.md.render()</code>
            reads CommonMark and the GitHub extensions an author expects: headings (<code>#</code> and underlined),
            emphasis, strong and strikethrough, code spans and fenced or indented blocks, quotes, nested, loose and
            numbered lists, task lists, tables with alignment, rules, links with a title, autolinks and bare
            addresses, reference links, images and videos, and hard and soft line breaks. Raw HTML is not read
            &mdash; every <code>&lt;</code> is text &mdash; so the output is built from a fixed set of elements and
            is safe to inject; <a href="#not-read">what is left out</a> is listed below.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Editor">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MdEditor" Title="Write and preview" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live below</strong> — type in it, use the toolbar or the keys, then switch to Preview.
            <code>.md-editor</code> is the toolbar, the textarea and the preview. The toolbar is yours: a button is
            any element with <code>data-md-cmd</code> (the <a href="#commands">commands</a> are listed below), and
            you write only the ones the field needs. The wiring is
            <code>data-md-view</code> on the root, <code>data-md-input</code> on the textarea,
            <code>data-md-preview</code> on the preview pane, and <code>data-md-tab</code> on each radio of the
            <a href="/segmented"><code>.segmented</code></a> view switch;
            <strong>the markup sits inert until <code>sednaUi.md.init()</code> runs</strong> — from C#,
            <code>ISednaUi.InitMarkdownAsync()</code> in <code>OnAfterRenderAsync</code>, which wires
            every editor in the document and is idempotent per editor. In Blazor, bind the textarea with
            <code>@@bind:event="oninput"</code> and let it own the value; toolbar edits dispatch a
            bubbling <code>input</code> event so the binding picks them up.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MdSplit" Title="Side by side" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live below.</strong> <code>data-md-view="split"</code> shows the text and its preview at once,
            the same height, the preview following the text as it scrolls. A third radio,
            <code>data-md-tab="split"</code>, lets the reader choose it; an editor that is never switched sets the
            attribute on the root and has no switch at all. Under 40rem the two stack.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MdFullscreen" Title="Full screen" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live below.</strong> <code>data-md-cmd="fullscreen"</code> on a button, written
            <code>aria-pressed="false"</code>: the editor fills the window, the page behind it becomes
            <code>inert</code> &mdash; neither the keyboard nor a screen reader reaches it &mdash; and
            <kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> or the button leaves. The editor stays where it is in the document, so the text is
            the same field and its value is not reset. Write, Preview and Split all work in it.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MdCount" Title="Word and character counts" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live below.</strong> An element inside the editor with <code>data-md-count="words"</code>,
            <code>"characters"</code> or <code>"lines"</code> gets that number as its text, and keeps it current. The
            number is all the script writes &mdash; the words around it are yours, in your language &mdash; and a
            <code>maxlength</code> on the textarea is the platform's own limit. Characters are code points, so an
            emoji counts once.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MdMedia" Title="Images and videos" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live below</strong> &mdash; switch to Preview. <code>![description](address)</code> is an image,
            and the same with the address of a video file &mdash; <code>.mp4</code>, <code>.m4v</code>,
            <code>.mov</code>, <code>.webm</code> or <code>.ogv</code> &mdash; is a video with controls that never
            plays by itself. <code>data-md-cmd="image"</code> and <code>"video"</code> wrap the selection as the
            description. Only <code>http</code>, <code>https</code> and relative addresses are kept; any other is
            written as its description. An app that uploads a picture inserts the text with the address it uploaded
            to, through <code>ISednaUi.InsertMarkdownAsync(textarea, text)</code> &mdash; the way <em>Inserting at the caret</em> below inserts a link.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.EditorField" Title="As a form field" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live below.</strong> The editor in place: a <code>.form-label</code> pointing at the
            textarea's own id, a <code>.form-hint</code> reached by <code>aria-describedby</code>, and the
            <a href="/form"><code>.form-actions</code></a> row. Label the textarea, not the
            <code>.md-editor</code> wrapper — the textarea is the control — and leave out the
            <code>data-md-cmd</code> buttons the field does not need.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.MarkdownLinkPicker" Title="Inserting at the caret" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live below</strong> — put the caret somewhere in the note, then link an order. The
            picker is the app's own <a href="/modal"><code>.modal</code></a>, awaited with
            <code>ShowModalAsync</code>, and <code>ISednaUi.InsertMarkdownAsync(textarea, text)</code>
            puts what it returns where the caret was: the selection is replaced, the caret lands after
            the text, focus returns to the textarea, and the bound value follows through the same
            <code>input</code> event a toolbar command fires. Assigning the bound field instead would
            put the text at the end. A textarea keeps its selection while a dialog has focus, so
            nothing has to be remembered across the picker. From script, it is
            <code>sednaUi.md.insert(textarea, text)</code>.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.MarkdownUpload" Title="Uploading pasted files" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live below</strong> &mdash; paste a picture from the clipboard, or drop a file on the field. Nothing
            is inserted: the editor sends <code>sedna-md-file</code>, bubbling and cancelable, with
            <code>{ files: [{ id, name, type, size }], index }</code>, and the app takes the files by cancelling it
            (<code>@@onsedna-md-file:preventDefault</code>). It reads each with
            <code>ISednaUi.OpenMarkdownFileAsync(textarea, id)</code> &mdash; from script,
            <code>sednaUi.md.file(textarea, id)</code> &mdash; uploads it, and answers with
            <code>InsertMarkdownAsync</code> and the address it got. Files nobody takes show the editor's
            <code>[data-md-file-notice]</code>. A paste that carries text as well is pasted as the text, and the browser
            never opens a dropped file in the tab. The editor keeps the last twenty files, so read them when the event
            arrives.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Reference">
    <h3 id="commands">Commands <CatAnchor Id="commands" /></h3>
    <p class="cat-lede">
        A <code>data-md-cmd</code> on any element inside the editor acts on the textarea's selection; script calls
        <code>sednaUi.md.apply(textarea, command)</code>. Every edit is typed through the browser, so one command is one
        step of its own undo, and it reaches the textarea as one <code>input</code> event, which
        <code>@@bind:event="oninput"</code> picks up. A read-only or disabled textarea is left alone.
    </p>
    <div class="sedna-scroll-x">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>bold</code>, <code>italic</code>, <code>strike</code>, <code>code</code></td><td>Marks the selection &mdash; with nothing selected, a word to type over &mdash; and takes the marks off a selection that has them, inside it or around it. Spaces at the ends of the selection stay outside the marks.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>h1</code> &hellip; <code>h6</code></td><td>Sets the level of every line the selection touches; the same level again takes it off. A menu of headings is the same: each item carries its command.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ul</code>, <code>ol</code>, <code>task</code>, <code>quote</code></td><td>Puts the marker on every line, in place of any other list marker, and takes it off when every line has it. A numbered list counts from 1.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>codeblock</code></td><td>Fences the selection on lines of its own, with a longer fence when the text holds one.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>hr</code>, <code>table</code></td><td>A block after the current line, with an empty line either side &mdash; a rule under a paragraph would otherwise underline it. The table's first heading is selected.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>link</code></td><td>A selection becomes the link's text with the address selected; a selected address stays, with the text selected.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>image</code>, <code>video</code></td><td><code>![description](https://)</code> around the selection, as above.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>indent</code>, <code>outdent</code></td><td>List items one level, as <kbd class="kbd">Tab</kbd> does.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>undo</code>, <code>redo</code></td><td>The browser's own history for the field.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>fullscreen</code></td><td>Full screen, as above; the button is <code>aria-pressed</code>.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3 id="keys">Keys <CatAnchor Id="keys" /></h3>
    <p class="cat-lede">
        While the textarea has focus. <kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> is <kbd class="kbd">Cmd</kbd> on a Mac.
        <code>data-md-keys="off"</code> on the editor turns all of this off.
    </p>
    <div class="sedna-scroll-x">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Keys</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">B</kbd>, <kbd class="kbd">I</kbd>, <kbd class="kbd">E</kbd>, <kbd class="kbd">K</kbd></td><td>Bold, italic, code, link.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">X</kbd></td><td>Strikethrough.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">7</kbd>, <kbd class="kbd">8</kbd>, <kbd class="kbd">9</kbd></td><td>Numbered list, bullet list, quote.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">Alt</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">1</kbd> &hellip; <kbd class="kbd">6</kbd>, <kbd class="kbd">C</kbd></td><td>A heading level, a code block.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd></td><td>At the end of a list item, task or quote line, goes on with the same marker &mdash; a numbered list counts on, a task starts unchecked. On an empty item it ends the list, one level at a time when it is nested. Inside a code block, in front of a marker, or with <kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd>, it is a newline.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Tab</kbd>, <kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">Tab</kbd></td><td>Indent and outdent the list items under the selection, and move through the cells of a table &mdash; <kbd class="kbd">Tab</kbd> in the last cell adds a row. <strong>Only where that is possible:</strong> on the first item of a list, on an item already a level under the one above, and on any line that is not a list item or a table row, Tab leaves the field as it does from any textarea, so the keyboard is never trapped.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Paste an address over a selection</td><td>The selection becomes a link to it. Anything else pasted is the browser's.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3 id="events-and-attributes">Events and attributes <CatAnchor Id="events-and-attributes" /></h3>
    <div class="sedna-scroll-x">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>data-md-view</code></td><td><code>write</code>, <code>preview</code> or <code>split</code> on the root; the script sets it, and an app may write it to start in one.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-md-fullscreen</code></td><td>On the root while the editor is full screen.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-md-dragging</code></td><td>On the root while a file is dragged over the textarea &mdash; the frame takes the focus ring.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-md-keys="off"</code></td><td>On the root: no shortcuts, no list or table keys.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-md-count</code></td><td><code>words</code>, <code>characters</code> or <code>lines</code> on an element inside the root: it holds that number.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-md-file-notice</code></td><td>An element inside the root, <code>hidden</code>, shown when a file is not taken.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>sedna-md-file</code></td><td><code>{ files: [{ id, name, type, size }], index }</code>, cancelable, on a file pasted or dropped. <code>@@onsedna-md-file</code>, a <code>SednaMarkdownFileEventArgs</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>the textarea's <code>input</code></td><td>One per edit, from the toolbar, a key or a paste, as a textarea sends it.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3 id="not-read">What it does not read <CatAnchor Id="not-read" /></h3>
    <div class="sedna-scroll-x">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Left out</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Raw HTML, inline and as a block, and HTML comments</td><td>The preview is a security boundary. An allow-list of tags is a sanitiser's job, done on the server; here every <code>&lt;</code> is text.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Footnotes, definition lists, math, emoji codes, <code>==highlight==</code>, <code>^super^</code></td><td>Not CommonMark, and each needs markup, an id or an engine the page may not have.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Heading ids and a table of contents</td><td>An id written into the page collides with the page's own; the app derives anchors from the text it renders.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Alert blocks (<code>&gt; [!NOTE]</code>), front matter</td><td>A convention of one host, not Markdown.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Reference definitions that span lines, and a link with <code>data:</code>, <code>javascript:</code> or any scheme beyond <code>http</code>, <code>https</code>, <code>mailto</code> and <code>tel</code></td><td>A link with another scheme is written as a dead link, and an image or video with one as its description.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Auto-closing brackets, Tab typing spaces in code, a button to tick a task in the preview</td><td>They change what a key does where the reader did not ask, trap the keyboard, or write to the text from a pane that only reads it.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout callout--danger">
        <i class="ri-shield-check-line"></i>
        <div class="callout-body">
            <span class="callout-title">Sanitise server-side as well</span>
            <code>sednaUi.md.render()</code> escapes all HTML <em>first</em> and only re-introduces a fixed
            set of constructs, with link hrefs restricted to <code>http:</code>, <code>https:</code>,
            <code>mailto:</code>, <code>tel:</code> and relative addresses, and image and video sources to <code>http:</code>,
            <code>https:</code> and relative ones. It is not a spec-complete parser: for untrusted
            input rendered to other users, sanitise server-side as well.
        </div>
    </div>
</CatGroup>

@code {
    // The one thing on this page that genuinely needs the circuit. An interop call
    // cannot run during prerendering, so it goes here rather than in OnInitializedAsync
    // — and the editor renders identically before it, which is the invariant this site
    // holds to: interactivity adds behaviour, it is never required to see an example.
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender) await Ui.InitMarkdownAsync();
    }
}
